# claude: port/ files that are portable across the POSIX-shaped GOOSes
# but must NOT be compiled for plan9 or windows at all -- selected here
# rather than #ifdef'd out inside themselves, so port/ stays free of
# GOOS branching (the Plan9 habit of choosing files in the build
# instead of branching in the source; cf. os/$GOOS/ and arch/$cputype/
# right below, which exist for exactly this reason).
#
# Each bridges a Plan9 API call onto POSIX syscalls that don't match it
# 1:1 -- seek() onto lseek(), remove() onto unlink()-or-rmdir() (Plan9's
# removes a directory too; POSIX's unlink refuses one), and dup(old,new)
# onto dup()-or-dup2() (Plan9 passes newfd == -1 to mean "lowest free").
# None of them is wanted where the Plan9 shape IS the native one: plan9's
# kernel provides seek(2)/remove(2)/dup(2) under exactly these names and
# semantics (syscall/os/plan9/svc_$cputype.s), and windows has no
# raw-syscall layer at all, so os/windows/open.c defines what it can
# itself on top of kernel32. Compiling them there would fail to resolve
# the POSIX primitives (which those GOOSes have no symbol for) or collide
# with the real definitions.
#
# mk evaluates this backquote when the mkfile is read, and a
# command-line 'GOOS=plan9' has already overridden the default above by
# then -- so callers need no override of their own, unlike
# SYSCALLOFILES=/OSEXTRAFILES= below, which windows must still pass
# explicitly.
PORTPOSIXOFILES=`{if(~ $GOOS plan9 windows) echo -n; if not echo port/seek.$O port/remove.$O port/dup.$O port/getenv.$O port/putenv.$O}

# claude: sbrk() and the per-GOOS brk() under it. Selected separately
# from PORTPOSIXOFILES above because the split is a DIFFERENT one: those
# files are "everywhere except plan9/windows", whereas port/sbrk.c is
# wanted exactly where a movable program break exists -- linux and plan9
# -- which is not the same set and not its complement either.
#
# port/sbrk.c is the portable bump-over-brk() half (see its own comment
# for why sbrk, not brk, is the primitive the toolchain codes against).
# It needs a brk() to call:
#   plan9   the kernel's own BRK syscall is already exactly the right
#           shape, so svc_$cputype.s supplies it directly, no glue.
#   linux   brk(2) exists but returns the new break instead of 0/-1, so
#           os/linux/brk.$O bridges it.
#   darwin  there is no usable brk at all (SYS_break=17 is vestigial and
#           fails on modern macOS), so port/sbrk.c is not built and
#           os/darwin/sbrk.$O provides a wholly different mmap(MAP_ANON)
#           -backed sbrk instead. Legitimate because sbrk's contract
#           never promised successive blocks abut each other -- verified
#           against all three in-tree callers (utilities/text/grep/sub.c,
#           utilities/byte/dd.c, linkers/8lk/compat.c), none of which
#           assume contiguity.
#   windows same as darwin: no break, so os/windows/sbrk.$O over
#           VirtualAlloc. Unlike dup(), nothing here is blocked by that
#           port's fd-as-truncated-HANDLE simplification -- no
#           descriptors are involved.
SBRKOFILES=`{if(~ $GOOS linux) echo port/sbrk.$O os/linux/brk.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS plan9) echo port/sbrk.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS darwin) echo os/darwin/sbrk.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS windows) echo os/windows/sbrk.$O; if not echo -n}

# claude: exits()/_exits() -- Plan9's process-termination pair
# (include/os/proc.h: "exits() is the libc exit that performs some
# cleanup ... while _exits() is the syscall that is more abrupt").
#
# plan9 already gets exits(char*) for free: syscall/os/plan9/
# svc_$cputype.s defines it directly as the raw EXITS syscall (the same
# file that supplies open/close/dup/brk/etc, see its own header
# comment) -- there is no separate raw-vs-cooked distinction to make
# there since the kernel call IS the primitive, so the only gap is
# _exits(), a one-line C alias for that same asm symbol
# (os/plan9/_exits.c).
#
# Every other GOOS has no native exits(char*) at all, and (unlike
# sbrk's per-OS split above) doesn't need per-OS glue either: this
# libc's own exit(int) is already a bare syscall/ExitProcess trampoline
# with no atexit-style cleanup phase to skip, so exits() and _exits()
# collapse to the exact same "map msg to 0/1, call exit()" logic on
# linux/darwin/windows alike -- one portable port/exits.c, not three
# os/$GOOS/ copies.
EXITSOFILES=`{if(~ $GOOS plan9) echo os/plan9/_exits.$O; if not echo port/exits.$O}

# claude: Tier 4 process control (docs/claude_notes/plan_syscalls.txt) --
# fork()/exec()/execl()/wait()/pipe(), the primitives compilers/pcc/pcc.c
# actually needs to fork+exec+wait for a subcommand (its own doexec()/
# dopipe()), i.e. the actual self-hosting milestone the plan doc names
# this tier for.
#
# all four GOOSes now, split four ways below rather than folded into
# PORTPOSIXOFILES above (that variable is "every POSIX-shaped GOOS
# except plan9/windows" -- close, but not quite the right split: linux
# and darwin genuinely share the SAME five files here, unlike
# PORTPOSIXOFILES's own members, which are all GOOS-independent
# already; and unlike PORTPOSIXOFILES, windows now DOES want a branch
# here, just a different one -- see below).
#
# linux and darwin share port/fork.c/exec.c/execl.c/wait.c/pipe.c
# UNCHANGED -- every one of those five files only ever calls a raw
# _sysfork()/_sysexecve()/_syswait4()/_syspipe() name (plus portable
# libc calls: malloc/free/va_list/_mainargv), never anything
# GOOS-specific, so the SAME C source works once syscall/os/darwin/
# supplies those four raw primitives under those exact names --
# _sysexecve/_syswait4 decl-generated normally (syscall_darwin_$cputype.decl),
# _sysfork/_syspipe as dedicated hand-written asm in svc_$cputype.s
# (XNU's classic BSD dual-register return convention -- isChild in a
# second register for fork, both fds in two registers for pipe, neither
# expressible through the generic _syscall6 trampoline's single `long`
# result -- see numbers_darwin_amd64.h's own citation of
# bsd/kern/syscalls.master, where both are marked NO_SYSCALL_STUB,
# meaning even Apple's own libSystem needs custom assembly for them).
# Unverified on real hardware -- no macOS host here, same epistemic
# status as the rest of os/darwin/ (see docs/claude_notes/
# notes_libc_selfhost.txt).
#
# plan9's own branch is genuinely different in SHAPE from linux/darwin's,
# not just its file list: RFORK/EXEC/AWAIT/PIPE (svc_$cputype.s) are
# already exactly Plan9-shaped (no POSIX-shape bridge needed, same
# story as open/dup/brk above), so exec() and pipe() need no C file at
# all here -- only fork() (built on rfork(), os/plan9/fork.c) and
# wait() (built on await(), os/plan9/wait.c, a genuinely different
# algorithm from port/wait.c's int-status decode -- Plan9's await(2)
# hands back a TEXT message, not a packed int) need one. execl.c is the
# one file plan9 actually SHARES with linux/darwin: it's pure portable
# C (only calls the portable exec() name, malloc/free, va_list), so it
# costs nothing to build for all three rather than copy it into
# os/plan9/ too.
#
# windows's own branch is different in KIND, not just shape: there is
# no execve(2)/fork(2)/wait4(2) analog at all, and no raw-syscall layer
# to bridge one through even if there were (same reason it's excluded
# from PORTPOSIXOFILES). os/windows/pipe.c (CreatePipe) is a real,
# faithful primitive with no compromise needed. os/windows/exec.c is a
# deliberately different design, not a bridge: CreateProcessA + a
# blocking WaitForSingleObject + GetExitCodeProcess + this process's own
# exit() -- see that file's own comment for why collapsing fork+exec+
# wait into that one call is the only way to keep exec()'s "never
# returns on success" contract honest without a real fork(). fork() and
# wait() are NOT approximated or stubbed here: fork() would need a
# Cygwin-style whole-system fixed-load-address scheme plus
# ReadProcessMemory/WriteProcessMemory address-space cloning to work at
# all (a different, much larger engineering project than a libc syscall
# wrapper), and wait() has nothing left to do once exec() already
# waited internally, in the same process, before this ever returns.
# compilers/pcc/pcc.c's actual `switch(fork()){case 0: exec(...)}`
# pattern therefore still cannot work end to end on windows -- see
# todo.org's own gap entry. execl.c is shared with linux/darwin/plan9
# for the same reason it already is there: pure portable C.
#
# $cputype riscv (rv32) is excluded up front too, same "checked first
# regardless of GOOS" style as STATOFILES's own riscv carve-out below:
# numbers_riscv.h's own Tier 4 comment explains why -- rv32 has no
# wait4(2) at all (64-bit-only in scripts/syscall.tbl), so none of
# _sysfork()/_sysexecve()/_syswait4()/_syspipe() were given a riscv
# backing this round. Leaving PROCOFILES non-empty for it would still
# `mk install` cleanly today (an unresolved symbol inside a .a archive
# member is silent until something actually links it in, and nothing
# does -- riscv is excluded from tests/c/hello_libc/fork.exe for the
# same reason), but would leave a libc.a that link-fails confusingly
# for the first real riscv caller instead of failing predictably here.
# claude: port/waitpid.$O added to both the linux/darwin and plan9
# branches below (not windows, which has no wait() to build it on) --
# unlike principia's own 9sys/waitpid.c (which reimplements await()'s
# text-parsing separately from wait()), this one is built directly on
# the portable wait() each branch already provides, so the exact same
# file works for both without a GOOS split of its own. Found blocking
# mk/Posix.c's Exit() while self-hosting mk.
# claude: port/spawn.$O (docs/claude_notes/notes_libc_api_design.txt's
# "spawn(): a portable process-spawn primitive" section) rides along
# with linux/darwin/plan9's own fork()+exec() -- it's built directly on
# both, so it belongs wherever they're already selected, not a
# separate conditional of its own. windows gets its own os/windows/
# spawn.c and os/windows/wait.c instead (real fork()-less CreateProcess
# story, see that section).
PROCOFILES=`{if(~ $cputype riscv) echo -n; if not if(~ $GOOS linux darwin) echo port/fork.$O port/exec.$O port/execl.$O port/wait.$O port/waitpid.$O port/pipe.$O port/spawn.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS plan9) echo os/plan9/fork.$O port/execl.$O os/plan9/wait.$O port/waitpid.$O port/spawn.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS windows) echo os/windows/cmdline.$O os/windows/exec.$O port/execl.$O os/windows/pipe.$O os/windows/spawn.$O os/windows/wait.$O port/waitpid.$O os/windows/fork.$O; if not echo -n}

# claude: Tier 6 notification (docs/claude_notes/plan_syscalls.txt,
# docs/claude_notes/notes_libc_api_design.txt's "Notes vs. signals").
# port/atnotify.c is pure portable C built only on notify()/noted()
# (both declared in include/os/plan9/note.h for every GOOS -- same
# "declare everywhere" convention PROCOFILES's own rfork/Waitmsg
# already use), so it's compiled for every GOOS that has, or will
# have, a real notify()/noted() -- plan9 (real syscalls, svc_$cputype.s
# above) and linux (os/linux/notify.c: sigaction()-based, modeled on
# plan9port's own BOOT/lib9/notify.c, see the design doc's "Notes vs.
# signals" section). darwin's own notify()/noted() is a real,
# documented gap, not just "unverified without hardware" like the rest
# of this tree's darwin work -- os/darwin/postnote.c's own header
# comment has the full story (XNU's raw sigaction(2) needs a userspace
# sa_tramp trampoline whose modern requirement is genuinely ambiguous
# from public sources, and there is no qemu-user Darwin emulation to
# fall back on the way every Linux arch had for this tier) -- so
# atnotify.$O is left in unresolved-until-linked there, same treatment
# PROCOFILES's own riscv carve-out already uses. postnote() itself
# needs none of that (just kill(2), confidently sourced) and IS
# implemented for darwin, same as plan9/linux: os/plan9/postnote.c (a
# write() to /proc/PID/note) for plan9, os/linux/notify.c's own
# kill()-based one bundled in for linux, os/darwin/postnote.c (a real
# 3-argument BSD kill(2), unlike either) for darwin. windows gets
# nothing: no notify-shaped primitive planned there.
NOTIFYOFILES=`{if(~ $GOOS linux) echo port/atnotify.$O os/linux/notify.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS darwin) echo port/atnotify.$O os/darwin/postnote.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS plan9) echo port/atnotify.$O os/plan9/postnote.$O; if not echo -n}

# claude: sigrestore.$O (arch/$cputype/sigrestore.s, Tier 6) holds
# sigentry -- the trampoline os/linux/notify.c installs as
# Ksigaction.handler on every arch, even the ones (386/arm/arm64)
# where it turns out to be a trivial tail-jump -- so unlike an earlier
# version of this comment assumed, mips is NOT excluded: it still
# needs sigentry (a real register-bridging one, since this compiler's
# own first-argument convention for mips uses R1, not the kernel's
# real $a0/R4 -- see arch/mips/sigrestore.s's own comment), even
# though that arch's kernel ABI has no sa_restorer FIELD at all
# (numbers_mips.h's own comment: "SA_RESTORER... was removed" from
# mips entirely) and so never needs the OTHER half (a real
# rt_sigreturn trampoline) any file here provides. Linux-only overall
# (this file has no reason to exist on plan9/darwin/windows).
SIGRESTOREOFILES=`{if(~ $GOOS linux) echo arch/$cputype/sigrestore.$O; if not echo -n}

# UNIX/POSIX-compat implementation code (fopen/fgets/fclose -- no
# Plan9-native equivalent at all, unlike strdup/seek/etc above, which
# stay in PORTOFILES) -- mirrors include/APE/'s own split of "ugly,
# must-be-UNIX-compatible" headers away from the rest of include/,
# kept out of port/ the same way for the same reason.
APEOFILES=\
	APE/stdio.$O\

SYSCALLOFILES=\
	syscall/os/$GOOS/svc_$cputype.$O\
	syscall/os/$GOOS/zsyscall_${GOOS}_$cputype.$O\

# the "os/$GOOS/" API-shape glue (Plan9's open()/include/os/file.h
# vs the raw POSIX-shaped _sysopen() syscall/ provides) -- kept as its
# own OFILES group, separate from SYSCALLOFILES above, matching the
# directory split lib_core/libc/os/ itself makes (see that dir's own
# arch/, os/ split mirroring syscall/'s). Arch-independent (unlike
# SYSCALLOFILES, which is per-cputype): open.c calls the same _sysopen()
# name regardless of which raw syscall backs it on a given arch.
# claude: per-GOOS glue beyond open.$O, selected here for the same
# reason PORTPOSIXOFILES is (choose files, don't #ifdef inside them).
# Only plan9 needs any so far, and for the mirror-image reason to
# PORTPOSIXOFILES's: access() is a real syscall on every other GOOS but
# an ordinary libc function on Plan9, which has no access(2) at all.
# Distinct from $OSEXTRAFILES below, which is set by the CALLER for
# cases the mkfile cannot know about (windows' per-arch kernel32 stubs).
# claude: extended for the "small tier" (getpid/getwd/time/nsec/sleep).
# Every GOOS now needs some of these, so this is no longer a plan9-only
# carve-out -- but the SPLIT differs per call, which is why they are
# grouped per-GOOS here rather than as one list per call:
#   getwd    a real syscall only on linux; darwin and plan9 both go via
#            a descriptor instead (fcntl(F_GETPATH), fd2path), windows
#            has GetCurrentDirectoryA.
#   getpid   a syscall on linux/darwin (generated straight into its
#            public name by the .decl, so no file here); an ordinary
#            file read on plan9; a kernel32 call on windows, where it
#            rides along in os/windows/time.c.
#   time/nsec/sleep
#            linux and darwin need glue to build the kernel's timespec/
#            timeval; plan9 reads /dev/bintime for time but has a real
#            SLEEP syscall needing no glue at all; windows converts the
#            FILETIME epoch.
# claude: os/plan9/putenv.c and os/windows/putenv.c added alongside
# their own getenv.$O, same "Tier 6 environ() round" as
# port/putenv.c's own addition to PORTPOSIXOFILES above -- see
# docs/claude_notes/notes_libc_api_design.txt's "Environment
# (getenv/putenv/environ)" entry. Both were declared in
# include/os/env.h from the start but had no implementation anywhere
# in this tree until now, on any GOOS.
OSGOOSOFILES=`{if(~ $GOOS plan9) echo os/plan9/access.$O os/plan9/getwd.$O os/plan9/getpid.$O os/plan9/time.$O os/plan9/getenv.$O os/plan9/putenv.$O os/plan9/convM2D.$O os/plan9/convD2M.$O os/plan9/dirread.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS linux) echo os/linux/getwd.$O os/linux/time.$O os/linux/isatty.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS darwin) echo os/darwin/getwd.$O os/darwin/time.$O os/darwin/isatty.$O os/darwin/dirread.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS windows) echo os/windows/getwd.$O os/windows/time.$O os/windows/getenv.$O os/windows/putenv.$O os/windows/dup.$O os/windows/isatty.$O os/windows/dirread.$O; if not echo -n}

# claude: dirfstat()/dirfwstat() (Tier 3, docs/claude_notes/plan_syscalls.txt).
# Unlike every other OSFILES entry, struct-stat layout is genuinely
# per-ARCH, not just per-GOOS -- so linux/darwin get a $cputype-suffixed
# file, the same way SYSCALLOFILES's svc_$cputype.$O is per-arch, rather
# than the arch-independent os/$GOOS/open.$O pattern every other call in
# this group follows. plan9 and windows don't need that split: only one
# or two arches are implemented there at all, and (unlike linux/darwin's
# raw kernel struct) the C-level glue itself doesn't vary by arch -- see
# os/plan9/stat.c's and os/windows/stat.c's own comments. riscv (rv32)
# gets no stat file at all, checked first regardless of GOOS since it
# only ever appears under linux here: numbers_riscv.h's own comment
# explains why (no fstat/newfstatat syscall on that arch, needs statx,
# not yet implemented) -- leaving STATOFILES empty for it is the file-
# selection way of tracking that gap, not a bug.
STATOFILES=`{if(~ $cputype riscv) echo -n; if not if(~ $GOOS plan9) echo os/plan9/stat.$O; if not if(~ $GOOS windows) echo os/windows/stat.$O; if not echo os/$GOOS/stat_$cputype.$O}

# claude: dirwstat() (by path) -- was port/dirwstat.c unconditionally
# (still is, for plan9/darwin/windows: plan9's dirfwstat() already
# renames natively via the wire protocol so the portable open+
# dirfwstat+close wrapper is enough there, and darwin/windows just
# don't support Dir.name-based renaming yet, an unchanged, still-
# documented gap). linux gets its own os/linux/dirwstat.c instead,
# arch-independent like os/plan9/stat.c, adding a real renameat2(2)
# call before the fallback open+dirfwstat+close -- found needed by
# utilities/files/mv.c, which dirfwstat() alone can't serve (no
# fd-based rename primitive on Linux). See os/linux/dirwstat.c's own
# header comment for the fuller story.
DIRWSTATOFILES=`{if(~ $GOOS linux) echo os/linux/dirwstat.$O; if not echo port/dirwstat.$O}

OSFILES=\
	os/$GOOS/open.$O\
	$OSGOOSOFILES\
	$STATOFILES\
	$DIRWSTATOFILES\
	$OSEXTRAFILES\

# $OSEXTRAFILES: same "leave unset, not assigned empty" convention as
# $ARCHEXTRAOFILES below, for an OS whose glue needs more than the one
# arch-independent os/$GOOS/open.$O every other GOOS gets away with.
# windows/amd64 is the first such case: os/windows/open.c's own header
# comment explains why there's no syscall/os/windows/ raw-syscall layer
# at all (kernel32.dll has no documented raw NT-syscall-number
# convention the way Linux/BSD do) -- its os/windows/winio_amd64.$O
# (the per-arch kernel32 stubs, plus exit()) has to be added here
# explicitly via 'OSEXTRAFILES=os/windows/winio_$cputype.$O' on the
# command line, same override style tests/c/hello_libc/mkfile's
# test_macos_amd64 already uses for RT0OFILE.

# $ARCHEXTRAOFILES: for arch-specific runtime support beyond rt0.s that
# only some arches need (e.g. arm has no hardware integer divide, so
# mkfiles/arm/mkfile sets this to arch/arm/div.$O -- the software
# _div/_divu/_mod/_modu helpers 5c calls into). Left unset (not
# assigned empty) everywhere else, same reasoning as CFLAGS_EXTRA
# above: mk must never see an explicit "ARCHEXTRAOFILES=" for archs
# that don't need it, so rc's own true-unset-vanishes-to-zero-words
# behavior applies instead of mk's eager one-empty-element-list one.
# RT0OFILE: arch/$cputype/rt0.s is genuinely OS-independent for every
# (GOOS, arch) pair *except* darwin/amd64 and (as of the linux argc/argv
# bridge below) darwin/arm64 -- dyld's LC_MAIN calling convention passes
# argc/argv in registers (RDI/RSI on amd64, X0/X1 on arm64), unlike a
# raw Linux ELF entry (argc/argv already on the stack, which is what
# 6c/7c-compiled code, including linux/amd64's and linux/arm64's own
# rt0.s, now explicitly bridges from -- see those files' own comments
# and docs/claude_notes/notes_debug_techniques.txt's "Cross-arch rt0.s
# bring-up" section). arm64's case is subtler than amd64's: X0 already
# matches 7c's own register-based first-argument convention for argc,
# by a real ABI coincidence (see docs/claude_notes/
# notes_libc_selfhost.txt) -- so darwin/arm64 needs a rt0_darwin.s not
# because dyld's convention differs in shape from 7c's own, but because
# linux/arm64's rt0.s bridge now *unconditionally* overwrites X0/X1 from
# the raw stack, which is wrong on darwin (no Linux-shaped argc/argv
# layout exists there to read). So amd64/rt0_darwin.s and
# arm64/rt0_darwin.s each exist as a second, GOOS-specific file (same
# shape as syscall/os/darwin/svc_$cputype.s needing its own file,
# above), selected by overriding this variable on the command line --
# see tests/c/hello_libc/mkfile's test_macos_amd64/test_macos_arm64 for
# the override.
RT0OFILE=arch/$cputype/rt0.$O

ARCHOFILES=\
	$RT0OFILE\
	$ARCHEXTRAOFILES\

OFILES=$FMTOFILES $UTFOFILES $MATHOFILES $PORTOFILES $PORTPOSIXOFILES $SBRKOFILES $EXITSOFILES $PROCOFILES $NOTIFYOFILES $SIGRESTOREOFILES $APEOFILES $SYSCALLOFILES $OSFILES $ARCHOFILES

HFILES=$TOP/include/libc.h $TOP/include/arch/$cputype/u.h

<$TOP/mkfiles/mksyslib

# after the include so mksyslib's own 'all' rule stays the default
# target (mk picks the *first* rule in the file when none is given on
# the command line)
#
# zsyscall_*.c is checked into the repo (unlike y.tab.[ch], which
# .gitignore deliberately excludes) -- this rule only regenerates it
# when the .decl spec it's derived from is newer, as a dev convenience.
# Committing it, like Go does for its own zsyscall_*.go, buys two
# things a truly-throwaway generated file (y.tab.c) doesn't need:
# (1) it's small, hand-checkable glue code worth reviewing in a diff,
# not opaque parser tables; (2) once numbers_$OS_$ARCH.h stops being
# hand-written and instead gets scraped from that OS's own kernel
# headers (the mksysnum_linux.sh-equivalent step noted as future work),
# generating it will require a host of that OS -- committing the
# output is what lets goken cross-compile syscall wrappers for an OS
# you don't have. After editing a .decl, regenerate and commit the
# diff in the same change.
# arm64/amd64/riscv64 need vlong-cast syscall args (see
# scripts/mksyscall.sh's own comment). This is NOT just a riscv64
# quirk, despite how it was first described here: every compiler in
# this tree defines `long` as 4 bytes (SZ_LONG in compilers/*c/gc.h),
# on every arch, including these three -- it's only the ones whose
# pointers are ALSO 4 bytes (386/arm/mips/riscv32: SZ_IND==SZ_LONG)
# that get away with the plain `long` prototype below. arm64 and
# amd64 have 8-byte pointers (SZ_IND=8) with that same 4-byte `long`,
# so they silently truncated pointer syscall args (e.g. write's buf)
# to 32 bits exactly like riscv64 -- found via real macOS execution of
# hello_libc (garbage in the upper 32 bits of a stack-passed argument
# slot XNU/dyld doesn't happen to leave zeroed, where a fresh Linux
# process stack did by luck), not via -S inspection; see
# docs/claude_notes/notes_libc_selfhost.txt for the write-up, including
# why this was masked on Linux this whole time. mips/riscv/386/arm's
# `long` is genuinely pointer-wide (SZ_IND==SZ_LONG==4 for all of
# them), so they're unaffected and keep the plain `long` prototype.
syscall/os/$GOOS/zsyscall_${GOOS}_$cputype.c:	syscall/os/$GOOS/syscall_${GOOS}_$cputype.decl
	if(~ $cputype riscv64 arm64 amd64)
		$TOP/scripts/mksyscall.sh $prereq vlong > $target
	if not
		$TOP/scripts/mksyscall.sh $prereq > $target
